// A top-level window — a surface the user sees, which a program creates and
// destroys with the tree. `title` names it; `favicon` is its icon. A window is
// not a destination: the pages a user goes between are sngl:ui/nav's `page`s,
// held by a `stack` the window shows, and the window around the stack is the
// chrome every page shares -- `title="Docs - {pages.current.title}"`.
//
// A window is an ordinary component and a member of `root`, which is what lets
// it stand at the root of a file. It has no body: every platform implements it
// in its own package, with a primitive of its own -- a toplevel on gtk4 and
// fyne, the one screen bubbletea and android draw, a document or a `<dialog>`
// on html -- so a target that renders one and does not is a build failure.
//
// `@error` is the window's outermost error boundary, and it always catches: a
// window whose call site handles no `@error` catches a raise from what it
// renders and drops it. Each platform writes it as a `boundary` around the
// content.
//
// `visible` is whether the window is on screen, on a target whose windows the
// program can show and hide: gtk4 and fyne, and bubbletea and android, which
// show one window and draw nothing while it is hidden. On html the first
// window that is always there is the document, which cannot leave the screen
// and is told of no close, so its `visible` and `@closed` do nothing; every
// other window is a `<dialog>` in it, shown and hidden as a desktop window is.
// It is a two-way prop: a window manager's close is the host reporting
// `visible = false`, the way a checkbox reports a click, and `@closed` fires
// after it. On bubbletea the close is ctrl+c, and on android the system back.
// So `:visible=shown` writes `shown` back, and a window nothing binds keeps the
// value in a cell of its own, which `open` and `close` below write. What the
// tree says decides what exists: a window under `if details` whose `@closed`
// writes `details = false` is destroyed, widgets and effects with it, and
// hiding one without destroying it is `visible`. A close that leaves no window
// on screen ends the program, unless it started with none.
component window(
    title string,
    favicon string,
    :visible bool = true,
    @error error,
    @closed(),
    content ...component node,
) root

// Puts the window on screen: `visible = true`, which is no change to a window
// already there, so it does not raise one. Reached through the window's `#id`
// -- `window #details(…)`, then `details.open()` from a handler -- which is
// what lets a program started with no window on screen, one whose `@run`
// never called `run`, bring one up in answer to something. The write lands on
// whatever `visible` is: the caller's var where `:visible=shown` bound one,
// the window's own cell where nothing did -- on html's document too, which
// stays on screen whatever its `visible` says.
func window.open(w window) {
    w.visible = true
}

// Takes the window off screen without ending the program: `visible = false`.
// `open` brings it back as it was.
func window.close(w window) {
    w.visible = false
}
